{"archetypes":[{"name":"state-machine","focus_area":"architecture","weight":1,"rationale":"Large new Python ship driver needs specialist scrutiny for orchestration, idempotency, and bash-path compatibility.","prompt_body":"Investigate the new Python ship driver as a state machine spanning checks, postbump, PR preparation, log flush, PR creation, CI monitoring, merge, and post-merge handling. Pay particular attention to phase ordering, re-entry after transient or needs-user outcomes, and whether the driver avoids duplicate push, duplicate post-merge flush, or prompt-side teardown responsibilities. Cite specific file paths and line ranges for any issues found, and follow the output-format rules from your outer wrapper exactly."},{"name":"bash-parity","focus_area":"risk-integration","weight":1,"rationale":"The change depends on preserving bash contracts while adding Python equivalents and selectors.","prompt_body":"Compare the Python-path behavior against the existing bash contracts described in the plan and touched scripts. Focus on exit-code mapping, skip conditions, state persistence expectations, finalize-state compatibility, and whether default bash behavior remains unchanged. Cite specific file paths and line ranges for any issues found, and follow the output-format rules from your outer wrapper exactly."},{"name":"runlog-integrity","focus_area":"correctness","weight":1,"rationale":"Run-log flushing and final-summary ordering are central acceptance criteria and easy to regress.","prompt_body":"Review changes around run-log pre-push and post-merge flushing, final-summary rendering, and manifest updates. Check state-file-less behavior, repo-root cwd requirements, status and PR-number ordering, and whether post-merge work can run zero, one, or multiple times under edge cases. Cite specific file paths and line ranges for any issues found, and follow the output-format rules from your outer wrapper exactly."},{"name":"ci-handback","focus_area":"risk-integration","weight":1,"rationale":"The Python path changes CI retry, rebase, and autonomous fixer handback routing.","prompt_body":"Inspect CI-monitor integration in the new driver and the Step 8+ prompt wiring for needs-user and transient outcomes. Focus on failed_run_id propagation, first-fixer and exhausted-fixer handbacks, rebase-only behavior on goto_rebase, retry caps, and whether user prompts are deferred until autonomous CI-fix paths are exhausted. Cite specific file paths and line ranges for any issues found, and follow the output-format rules from your outer wrapper exactly."},{"name":"teardown-state","focus_area":"security","weight":1,"rationale":"Finalize and teardown paths manipulate tmpdirs, branches, issue titles, and persisted state from mixed sources.","prompt_body":"Review finalize and teardown-related changes for safe handling of tmpdir paths, session identifiers, branch cleanup, issue renames, and state loaded from ship-pr-state or finalize-state files. Pay close attention to path traversal guards, stale or missing state fallbacks, repo-unavailable and forked behavior, and whether stalled runs preserve artifacts correctly. Cite specific file paths and line ranges for any issues found, and follow the output-format rules from your outer wrapper exactly."},{"name":"ci-harness","focus_area":"risk-integration","weight":1,"rationale":"The diff wires new pytest-based parity coverage into existing harness shards and requirements.","prompt_body":"Examine the Makefile, workflow requirements, and parity-test additions for whether the intended tests actually run in CI and locally. Check target registration, shard wiring, pytest availability, skip conditions, and whether new tests assert the critical acceptance criteria rather than only mocked happy paths. Cite specific file paths and line ranges for any issues found, and follow the output-format rules from your outer wrapper exactly."}]}
